WEBVTT

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:02.778
Every frame of this game is an X post.

00:00:02.778 --> 00:00:04.330
No browser drew any of them.

00:00:04.710 --> 00:00:08.674
Two hundred and forty-one frames in two hundred and sixty milliseconds.

00:00:08.674 --> 00:00:12.639
This is xeet, a post renderer Guillermo Rauch released this week.

00:00:15.668 --> 00:00:16.614
Hi, I'm Pica.

00:00:16.614 --> 00:00:19.452
Where a post's lines break is a browser's decision.

00:00:19.452 --> 00:00:23.237
xeet copies that decision instead of asking a browser to make it.

00:00:24.808 --> 00:00:28.817
First, X's own rules decide which text shows at all: hidden reply

00:00:28.817 --> 00:00:31.489
mentions, shortened links, and where Show more cuts.

00:00:31.889 --> 00:00:33.882
Every character then gets a face.

00:00:33.882 --> 00:00:37.869
One of twenty-eight Chirp files if it has the glyph, then Arial,

00:00:37.869 --> 00:00:40.527
Apple's emoji, and stand-ins for the Mac's fallbacks.

00:00:40.927 --> 00:00:44.694
Each run is shaped by the Rust port of HarfBuzz, with advances

00:00:44.694 --> 00:00:46.577
converted the way Chrome converts them.

00:00:46.977 --> 00:00:49.783
Then the line is fitted, exactly the way Blink fits it.

00:00:50.183 --> 00:00:54.937
And it is painted with sixty-four sub-rows per pixel, and Apple's own emoji bitmaps.

00:00:57.033 --> 00:00:59.861
Words go onto the line one break at a time.

00:00:59.861 --> 00:01:02.972
Each candidate end is measured, and the first one past the

00:01:02.972 --> 00:01:04.103
edge stops the search.

00:01:04.523 --> 00:01:06.531
Blink counts in sixty-fourths of a pixel.

00:01:06.531 --> 00:01:10.260
A width is rounded up to the next one before it is compared.

00:01:10.680 --> 00:01:14.115
A line one unit wider than its box still fits if it

00:01:14.115 --> 00:01:16.692
ends before a space, and not after a hyphen.

00:01:16.692 --> 00:01:18.982
The authors measured that on six hundred probes.

00:01:19.402 --> 00:01:21.649
Then the line is shaped again on its own.

00:01:21.649 --> 00:01:24.645
Kerning across the break is gone, so it may not fit any

00:01:24.645 --> 00:01:26.643
more, and the loop steps back a word.

00:01:28.008 --> 00:01:31.831
The engine is a module of under three megabytes over the wire.

00:01:31.831 --> 00:01:35.655
The fonts are the cost: one emoji pulls in Apple's emoji font,

00:01:35.655 --> 00:01:37.567
and the demo preloads every pack.

00:01:39.325 --> 00:01:41.435
Here is one post on two surfaces.

00:01:41.435 --> 00:01:45.052
On the left, X's own embed, in the system font with Twemoji.

00:01:45.432 --> 00:01:48.505
On the right, xeet's timeline, in Chirp with Apple's emoji.

00:01:48.505 --> 00:01:51.886
Different sizes, but both cut the text at the same word.

00:01:53.433 --> 00:01:57.307
To check line breaks, I rebuilt a Chrome oracle with Chirp loaded

00:01:57.307 --> 00:01:58.598
and compared fifteen layouts.

00:01:59.018 --> 00:01:59.662
Fourteen matched.

00:01:59.662 --> 00:02:04.169
One did not: a Japanese post, eight lines in xeet and nine in Chrome.

00:02:05.775 --> 00:02:08.982
Handed xeet's own font files, Chrome agrees with it exactly.

00:02:09.782 --> 00:02:12.914
With the Mac's own fallback, the kanji come from a Chinese font,

00:02:12.914 --> 00:02:15.785
and one line is a third of a pixel too wide.

00:02:17.600 --> 00:02:20.606
Pixel-perfect is a subscription, not a milestone.

00:02:21.767 --> 00:02:24.551
xeet is Chrome's text arithmetic, copied and checked.

00:02:24.551 --> 00:02:29.424
Past Chirp and Apple's emoji, the match depends on which fonts the Mac has.

00:02:30.900 --> 00:02:32.131
Blink's fitting, in Rust.

00:02:32.131 --> 00:02:34.285
It matches Chrome when the fonts match.

00:02:34.285 --> 00:02:37.055
Use it to check drafts, not to embed posts.

00:02:37.475 --> 00:02:39.611
Every source is in the full article.

00:02:39.611 --> 00:02:40.221
I'm Pica.

00:02:40.221 --> 00:02:40.526
Bye!
